WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:01.950
Two apartments.

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.730
The same advertised rent:
80,000 dirhams a year.

3
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.354
Rami is thinking of buying one to rent out.

4
00:00:08.354 --> 00:00:11.527
He assumes the same rent
means the same income.

5
00:00:11.617 --> 00:00:13.223
Then the building bills arrive.

6
00:00:13.223 --> 00:00:17.401
In Dubai, the owner pays
the service charges, not the tenant.

7
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:20.265
In this fictional example, one apartment has

8
00:00:20.265 --> 00:00:23.581
12,000 dirhams a year
in service charges.

9
00:00:23.581 --> 00:00:25.601
The other has 20,000.

10
00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:27.901
Subtract just that one cost.

11
00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:32.041
80,000 minus 12,000
leaves 68,000.

12
00:00:32.041 --> 00:00:35.681
80,000 minus 20,000
leaves 60,000.

13
00:00:35.871 --> 00:00:38.682
Same rent, but 8,000 dirhams apart.

14
00:00:38.682 --> 00:00:43.181
And that’s before vacancy, repairs,
financing and every other cost.

15
00:00:43.271 --> 00:00:45.181
So neither number is profit.

16
00:00:45.181 --> 00:00:47.381
It’s only what’s left after one bill.

17
00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:50.674
Service charges pay for running
the shared building:

18
00:00:50.674 --> 00:00:53.441
things like security, cleaning, maintenance,

19
00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:56.465
shared utilities, insurance and reserves.

20
00:00:56.555 --> 00:01:00.985
In Dubai, they’re approved by
the Real Estate Regulatory Agency,

21
00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:03.485
and they differ from one project to another.

22
00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:05.460
The Dubai Land Department says

23
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.885
an owner’s share is based on
the unit’s area

24
00:01:07.885 --> 00:01:10.374
in the title deed,
using the approved rate

25
00:01:10.374 --> 00:01:12.692
from its service charge index.

26
00:01:12.782 --> 00:01:15.812
The index asks for the community, the project,

27
00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:18.072
the type of use and the budget year.

28
00:01:18.072 --> 00:01:21.552
So check the figures for the right building
and the right year.

29
00:01:21.552 --> 00:01:25.058
Don’t copy a rate from another building
and assume it’s yours.

30
00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:28.318
Before you compare deals,
put the same rent assumption,

31
00:01:28.318 --> 00:01:32.858
the approved building charges
and your other regular costs side by side.

32
00:01:32.948 --> 00:01:35.038
A beautiful lobby can be worth it.

33
00:01:35.038 --> 00:01:37.687
Its bill still belongs in the comparison.

34
00:01:37.977 --> 00:01:39.447
Ahmadreza Samadi.

35
00:01:39.447 --> 00:01:41.384
More at a-samadi.com
